## 3. Integer Caching

### Simple Definition

CPython caches/reuses some commonly used integer objects (typically -5 to 256) instead of creating a new integer object every time.

### Example

```python
a = 10
b = 10

print(a == b)
print(a is b)

```

**Output:**

```text
True
True

```

Both names refer to the same cached integer object.

### Point-wise

* Integers are immutable.
* CPython keeps some integer objects ready for reuse.
* When the same cached integer is needed, Python reuses the existing object, saving object creation time and memory.
* The exact caching behavior is an implementation detail, so don't write programs that depend on it.

**Important:**
Don't use `if x is 10:`. Use `if x == 10:` because `==` compares value, while `is` compares object identity.

> **Easy memory:** Integer caching = CPython reuses some frequently used integer objects.

