## 4. CPython vs PyPy

First, understand this: **Python** is the programming language. **CPython** and **PyPy** are implementations of Python.

| Feature | CPython | PyPy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Status** | Most commonly used Python implementation | Alternative Python implementation |
| **Language** | Mainly written in C | Implemented using RPython |
| **Execution** | Traditional interpreter execution model | Has a JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler |
| **Optimization** | `.pyc` caching is used | Runtime/JIT optimization is a major feature |
| **Compatibility** | Very broad package compatibility | Compatibility can differ for some C-extension packages |

### What does JIT mean?

Suppose a loop runs millions of times. PyPy's JIT identifies this frequently executed code and optimizes it into fast machine code *while the program is running*.

**Important:** Do not assume "PyPy is always faster than CPython." JIT helps immensely with long-running, computation-heavy pure-Python code, but performance always depends on the specific program.

> **Easy memory:** CPython = standard/common implementation. PyPy = alternative implementation with JIT optimization.

